H1.body:
aDyayanaM [i-lyuw] Learning, study, reading (especially the Vedas); one of the six duties of a Brāhmaṇa. The study of the Vedas is allowed to the first
3 classes, but not to a Śūdra Ms. 1. 88-91. aDyayanaM ca akzaramAtrapAWa iti vEdikAH, sArTAkzaragrahaRamiti mImAMsakAH; the latter view is obviously correct;
cf. yaTA paSurBAravAhI na tasya Bajate PalaM . dvijastaTArTAnaBijYo na vedaPalamaSnute .. or better still, Yāska's Nirukta: sTARurayaM BArahAraH kilABUdaDItya vedaM na vijAnAti yo'rTaM . arTajYa it (arTavid) sakalaM BadramaSnute nAkameti jYAnaviDUtapApmA .. See also under anagni.

anizpatraM
ind. [niHsftaM patraM pakzo yatra tAdfSaM na Bavati] So that the arrow (the feathery portion of it) does not come out on the other side;
i. e. not with great force.

anuyuktin
a. [astyarTe ini] One who has ordered, examined;
cf. gfhItin, aDItin.

anfRa
a. Free from debt, who has paid off the debt (due to another) with
gen. of person or thing; enAmanfRAM karomi Ś. 1; tatrAnfRAsmi U. 7; prARErdaSaraTaprIteranfRaM (gfDraM) R. 12. 54; Mv. 5. 58; pitFRAmanfRaH Ms. 9. 106; 6. 94. Every one that is born has three debts to pay off:-to Sages, Gods, and the Manes;
cf. jAyamAno vE brAhmaRastriBirfRavA jAyate brahmacaryeRarziByaH, yajYena deveByaH, prajayApitfByaH; he, therefore, who learns the Vedas, offers sacrifices to Gods, and begets a son, becomes anfRa (free from debt); eza vAnfRaH yaH putrI yajvA brahmacArivAsI;
cf. also fzidevagaRasvaDABujAM SrutayAgaprasavEH sa pArTivaH . anfRatvamupeyivAnbaBO pariDermukta ivozRadIDitiH R. 8. 30.

apAM enters into several compounds;
e. g. apAMsaMvartaH destruction of the world by water; apAMvatsaH ‘calf of waters’,
N. of a star; apAMnapAt, °naptA, °napttriya, °garBa, apAnnapAt, aponapAt
Ved.
N. of Agni or fire as sprung from water;
cf. Ms. 9. 321; apAMpatiH, niDiH nATaH, appatiH lord of waters, the ocean;
N. of Varuṇa; apAMpittaM, appittaM fire.

apaWa
a. Unable to read; not reading; a bad reader;
cf. apaca.

aputrikA The daughter of a sonless father, who herself has no male child; one who is not appointed by her father to beget male issue for him on failure of a son;
cf. akftA.
— kaH The father of such a daughter.

arvuka [arv-hiMsaneM bAhu° ukaY]
N. of a tribe or people in the south mentioned in the Mahābhārata and conquered by Sahadeva;
N. of kings living in the southern forest.

avasAyin
a. Residing, dwelling in;
cf. aMtyAvasAyin.

AdAya
ind. Having taken;
oft. with a prepositional force ‘with;’ jAlamAdAya H. 1.

IMt 1
P. To bind;
cf. at.

udDyaH [P. III. 1. 115;
cf. ujJatyudakamiti udDyaH Malli.]
N. of a river; toyadAgama ivodDyaBidyayoH R. 11. 8.

upacakzus
n. [upagataM cakzurivaH] An eye-glass, spectacles;
cf. upanetraM.

upanAgarikA A variety of vfttyanuprAsa. It is formed by sweet-sounding letters (mADuryavyaMjakavarRa);
e. g.
cf. the example cited in K. P. 9; apasAraya GanasAraM kuru hAraM dUra eva kiM kamalEH . alamalamAli mfRAlEriti vadati divAniSaM bAlA ...

upAnah
f. [upa-nah-kvip upasargadIrGa] A sandal, shoe; upAnadgUQapAdasya sarbA carmavfteva BUH H. 1. 142; Ms. 2. 246; SvA yadi kriyate rAjA sa kiM nASnAtyupAnahaM H. 3. 58;
cf. ‘What is bred in the bones cannot go out of flesh’, or ‘Habit is second nature’.

uBa
pron. a. (Used only in the dual) Both uBO tO na vijAnItaH Bg. 2. 19; Ku. 4. 43; Ms. 2. 14; Śi. 3. 8. [cf Zend. uba;
Gr. amphi;
L. ambo].

UravyaH (vyA
f.) [Uru-yat] A Vaiśya, a man of the third tribe (as born from the thighs of Brahmā or Puruṣa);
cf. Ms. 1. 31, 87.

erakA A kind of grass (said to have turned to clubs when plucked by Kṛṣṇa and his family;
cf. Mb. mOsalaparvan).
— kaM A woollen carpet.

kawaMBara
N. of the kawaBI tree.
— rA
N. of several plants:
1 nAgabalA;
2 prasAriRI;
3 rohiRI;
4 hastinI;
5 kalaMbikA;
6 mUrvA.

kanana
a. One-eyed;
cf. kARa.

kAkzaH [kutsitamakzaM atra, koH kAdeSaH;
cf. P. VI. 3. 104] A side-long look, a glance.
— kzaM Frown, look of displeasure, malicious look; kAkzeRAnAdarekzitaH Bk. 5. 24.

kAsAraH, -raM A pond, pool, lake;
(
pb) Bv. 1. 43; Bh. 1. 32, Gīt. 2.

kuMj 1
P. (kuMjati) To murmur;
cf. kUj.

kuMqalanA Encireling (as a word) to denote that it is to be left out or not considered; tadojasastadyaSasaH sTitAvimO vfTeti citte kurute yadA yadA . tanoti BAnoH parivezakEtavAttadA viDiH kuMqalanAM viDorapi .. N. 1. 14;
cf.
2 95 also.

kftvas
ind. An affix added to numerals to denote ‘fold’ or ‘times’;
e. g. azwakftvaH eight times, eightfold; so daSa°, paMca° &c.

kedara
a. Squint-eyed;
cf. kekara.

KAdatamodatA [KAdata modaDvamiti satataM yatrABiDIyate] Eating and being glad;
cf. “Eat, drink and be merry”; so KAdatavamatA, KAdatAcamatA;
cf. P. II. 1. 72.

gurviRI, gurvI A pregnant woman;
e. g. gurviRIM nAnugacCati na spfSaMti rajasvalAM.
— rvI The wife of a preceptor.

gfBaM
Ved. A house;
cf. gfhaM.

gez 1
A. (gezate, gezRa) To seek, search, investigate;
cf. gavez.

cAturmAsyaM [caturzu mAsezu Bavo yajYaH, Rya]
N. of a sacrifice performed every four months;
i. e. at the beginning of kArtika, PAlguna and AzAQa.

jozA, jozit
f. A woman;
cf. yozA, yozit.

JF 4 9
P. (JIryati or JfRAti) To grow old;
cf. jF.

talit
f. Lightning;
cf. taqit.

tIk 1
A. (tIkate) To go, move;
cf. wIk.

tukKAraH, tuKAraH, tuzAraH
N. of a people inhabiting the Vindhya mountain;
cf. Vikr. 18. 93.

dfMPU
f. A snake in general;
cf. dfnPU.

do 4
P. (dyati, dita;
caus. dApayati;
desid. ditsati)
1 To cut, divide.
2 To mow, reap. -WITH ava to cut or lop off; yadanyasminyajYe srucyavadyati Śat. Br.

drE 1
P. (drAyati) To sleep;
cf. drA.

DanvaMtariH
N. of the physician of the gods, said to have been produced at the churning of the ocean with a cup of nectar in his hand;
cf. caturdaSaratna.

DyE 1
P. (DyAyati, DyAta;
desid. diDyAsaMti;
pass. DyAyate) To think of, meditate upon, ponder over, contemplate, reflect upon, imagine, call to mind; DyAyato vizayAn puMsaH saMgastezUpajAyate Bg. 2. 62; na DyAtaM padamISvarasya Bh. 3. 11; pitFn DyAyan Ms. 3. 224; DyAyaMti cAnyaM DiyA Pt. 1. 136; Me. 3; Ms. 5. 47, 9. 21.

DvaR 1
P. (DvaRati) To sound;
cf. Dvan.

nivacane
ind. Not speaking, ceasing to speak, holding the tongue (regarded as a gati or preposition, or a separate word, when used with kf;
e. g. nivacanekftya, nivacane kftvA; P. I. 4. 76).

pawaccaraH A thief;
cf. pAwaccara.
— raM Old or ragged clothes.

parikartf
m. A priest who performs the marriage ceremony of a younger brother whose elder brother is not yet married; parikartA yAjakaH Hārīta;
cf. parivettf.

paridAyin
m. A father who gives his daughter in marriage to a man whose elder brother is not yet married;
cf. parivettf.

pF 3 9
P. (pirpIMta, pfRAti, papAra, apArIt, pari-rI-zyati, pari-rI-tuM, pUrRa;
pass. pUryate;
caus. pUrayati-te;
desid. pipari-rI-zati, pupUrzati)
1 To fill, fill up, complete.
2 To fulfil, gratify (as hopes &c.).
3 To fill with wind, blow (as a conch, flute &c.).
4 To satisfy, refresh, please; pitFnapArit Bk. 1. 2
5 To rear, bring up, nourish, nurture, cherish.

pyAna
a. Fat, grown fat;
cf. pIna.

pracC 6
P. (pfcCati, papracCa, aprAkzIt, prakzyati, prazwuM, pfzwa;
caus. pracCayati;
pass. pfcCyate;
desid. pipracCizati)
1 To ask, question, interrogate, inquire of (with two
acc.); papracCa rAmAM ramaRoBilAzaM R. 14. 27; Bk. 6. 8; R. 3. 5; Bg. 2. 7; brAhmaRaM kuSalaM pfcCet Ms. 2. 127; so mahASvetA kAdaMbarImanAmayaM papracCa K. 192; kuSalamabale pfcCati tvAM viyuktaH Me. 104; R. 1. 58.
2 To ascertain, learn by inquiry.
3 To seek, seek for.

pratinirdeSya
a. That which, though before expressed, is repeated in order to state something more about it;
cf. the instance given in K. P. 7; udeti savitA tAmrastAmra evAstameti ca, where tAmra is repeated to show that the sun that rises red sets also red.

pralaB 1
A. To cheat, deceive;
cf. vipralaB.

Bfj 1
A. (Barjate) To roast, fry;
cf. Brasj.

Brasj 6
U. (Bfjjati, Bfzwa;
caus. Barjayati-te, Brajjayati te;
desid. biBarkzatibiBrakzati, biBarjizati biBrajjizati) To fry, roast, parch, broil; (
fig. also); baBrajja nihati tasmin Soko rAvaRamagnivat Bk. 14. 86.

maruttaH
N. of a king of the solar race, who is said to have performed a sacrifice in which the Gods took the part of waiters &c.;
cf. tadapyeza Sloko'BigIto marutaH parivezwAro maruttasyAvasan gfhe . Avikzitasya kAmaprerviSvedevAH saBAsada iti ..

mfDaM War, battle, fight;
(
pb) sattvavihitamatulaM Bujayorbalamasya paSyata mfDe' DikupyataH Ki. 12. 39; R. 13. 65; Mv. 5. 13.

raMGas
f. Speed, haste;
cf. raMhas.

rAtriMmanya
a. Looking like night (as a cloudly or dark day);
cf. rajaniMmanya.

ruMw 1
P. (ruMwati) To rob, steal;
cf. luMw.

lakzaSas
ind. By hundreds of thousands;
i. e. in large numbers.

laC 1
P. (lacCati) To mark, see;
cf. lakz.

lajj 6
A. (lajjate, lajjita) To be ashamed, to blush;
cf. lasj.

laS 10
U. (lASayati-te) To exercise or practise any art;
cf. las.

luh 1
P. (lohati) To covet, desire or long for;
cf. luB.

vawuH
1 A boy, lad, youth, stripling;
oft. used like the English word ‘chap’ or ‘fellow’; capaloyaM vawuH Ś. 2; nivAryatAmAli kimapyayaM vawuH punarvivakzuH sPuritottarADaraH Ku. 5. 83;
cf. bawu also.
2 A religious student or Brahmacārin
q. v.

vF 9
U. (vfRAti, vfRIte, vUrRa;
pass. vUryate;
desid. vuvUrzati-te or vivarizati-te
(
pb) or vivarIzati-te) To choose, select; (see vf I.)

velliH
f. A creeper;
cf. valli.

vye 1
U. (vyayati-te, Uta;
caus. vyAyayati-te;
desid. vivyAsati)
1 To cover.
2 To sew.

vraSc 6
P. (vfScati, vfkRa;
caus. vraScayati-te;
desid. vivraScizati or vivrakzati)
1 To cut, cut up or asunder, tear, lacerate.
2 To wound.

SaMtanuH
N. of a king of the lunar race. He married Gaṅgā and Satyavatī; by the former wife he had a son named Bhīṣma, and by the latter Citrāṅgada and Vicitravīrya. Bhīṣma remained a celibate all his life, and his younger brothers died childless;
cf. BIzma.

SarmizWA
N. of one of the wives of Yayāti and daughter of Vṛshaparvan, the king of the Daityas;
cf. devayAnI and yayAti.

SAkawAyanaH
N. of a philologist and grammarian often referred to by Pāṇini and Yāska;
cf. vyAkaraRe Sakawasya ca tokaM Nir.

SutudriH -drUH
f. The river Sutlej;
cf. Satadru.

SrOzaw
ind. An exclamation or formula used in making an offering with fire to the gods or departed spirits;
cf. vazaw or vOzaw.

SloR 1
P. (SloRati) To heap together, collect, gather;
cf. SroR.

saMjayaH
N. of the charioteer of king Dhṛtarāṣṭra. He tried to bring about a peaceful settlement of the dispute between the Kauravas and Pāṇḍavas, but failed. It was he who narrated the events of the great Bhāratī war to the blind king Dhṛtarāṣṭra;
cf. Bg. 1. 1.

saMPewaH An angry or tumultuous conflict, an incident describing the mutual encounter of angry persons; see S. D. 379, 420;
e. g. the encounter between mADA and aGoraGaMwa. in Māl. act
5

sudi
ind. In the bright fortnight of a lunar month;
cf. vadi.

stF 9
U. (stfRAti, sfRIte, stIrRa;
desid. tistari rI-zati-te, tistIrzati-te) To cover, strew &c.; see stf.

sPawaH A snake's expanded hood;
cf. Pawa-wA.
— wI Alum.

hahA
m. A kind of Gandharva;
cf. hAhA.

hevAkaH Ardent or intense desire, eagerness; (this word, like the word lawaBa
q. v., is used only by later writers like Kalhaṇa, Bilhaṇa, and is probably derived from Persian or Arabic); asminnAsIttadanu niviqASlezahevAkalIlAvelladvAhukvaRitavalayA saMtataM rAjalakzmIH Vikr. 18. 101;
cf. hevAkin below.
